<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#imperial-aramaic">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imperial Aramaic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th–4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Egyptian Aramaic, Official Aramaic, Standard Aramaic, Achaemenid Aramaic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> is a term coined by modern scholars for a historical variety of Aramaic, also called Egyptian Aramaic because most surviving examples were found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In its wider sense it names the phase from the middle of the 8th century BCE to the end of the 4th century BCE, when Aramaic served as a language of public life and administration in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>; others restrict it to the Achaemenid period. The Elephantine papyri and ostraca are its largest group of records. Its script gave rise to the Aramaic-derived alphabets of the East, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, Palmyrene and Mandaic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imperial-aramaic.jpg" alt="Imperial Aramaic" width="330" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Panegyrics of Granovetter · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Letoon_stele.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Royal Road's courier system enabled the king to communicate with the furthest corners of the empire within days, and <b>Imperial Aramaic</b> served as the administrative lingua franca across the satrapies regardless of local languages.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars distinguish Old Aramaic, of the early inscriptions; <b>Imperial Aramaic</b>, the standard language of the Persian empire; the Middle Aramaic dialects of the early centuries CE, among them Jewish Palestinian Aramaic, Nabataean, Palmyrene, Syriac, Jewish Babylonian Aramaic and Mandaic; and the Neo-Aramaic languages spoken today.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>Middle Persian was written in abjads, scripts that write mainly the consonants, derived from the <b>Imperial Aramaic</b> alphabet.</blockquote><a href="/language/middle-persian">Middle Persian · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>The Achaemenid Empire then made <b>Imperial Aramaic</b> its main language of administration from Egypt to Bactria, as the papyri of the Jewish garrison at Elephantine show, while Old Persian was used almost only for royal monuments such as Darius's trilingual inscription at Behistun.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li></ul></section>
